e you aid  including basic necessities like food  and shelter  once a word of out lorry had been  declared life was  extremely rough the crazy history of  outlawy extends all the way back to  ancient roman laws of exile  the penalty was then referred to as  homosacchar latin for  the accursed man and meant as with  medieval society the unlucky individual  was forced from civilization and  deprived of all civic rights  exile also included the related  punishment of aqua  at igni interdictus literally  translating as  to forbid water and fire this opposing  addendum meant what it implies codifying  the illegality of providing outlaws with  basic necessities  pretty similar to medieval laws around  offering aid  examining the history of outlawary and  other countries in early germanic law  the death penalty is  notably absent altogether with being  made an outlaw the most severe  punishment  since the label was so dangerous this  makes a grim amount of sense  being made an outlaw was essentially a  fate worse than death  much later in the 16th century the  german concept of  vogel fry took on a similar meaning the  denotation of the term is similar to  free as a bird which began as a positive  description implying independence  with time however being a vogel fry  meant you had the same  legal rights as a bird or beast due to  criminal actions  and as with other cultures meant your  days were numbered  exactly what constitutes a crime serious  enough to be made in outlaw depended on  the culture and time period with  everything from  robbery to murder to not showing up for  court when summoned being listed as  examples  the legendary outlaw of english folklore  robin hood  might have certainly faced such a  punishment were he a real historical  figure in 13th or 14th century ce  making the character a bit more hardcore  than he's often remembered  a notable historic outlaw is norse  warrior eric the red  who is exiled in 982 ce for killing  another member of his community  not interested in sticking around to be  potentially killed while an outlaw he  sailed north and might have helped  discover  greenland pretty interesting stuff  going full circle and returning to  english common law the outlaw punishment  is thankfully no longer in effect in the  modern day  funnily enough though the law wasn't  officially abolished in england until as  late as 1938  although the concept was already  obsolete and wasn't actually being used  still there's something interesting  about the fact that outlaw punishment  technically survived all the way up  until the invention of the car  were the punishment about lorry brought  back today it would  be extremely terrifying in our modern  society so we can all be glad we don't  live  in the medieval era and that's where a  discussion of ancient outlaws reaches  its conclusion  i hope you enjoyed learning about a law  i think we can all agree  wouldn't be too nice to have applied to  you please consider subscribing and  leaving this video a like if you enjoyed  the content  thank you so much for watching and i'll  see you in the next video  [Music]  you

